Building the Debian package
===========================

This file is not installed into the binary package; `README.source` is the
conventional name for instructions aimed at whoever builds the source package.

What this is, and is not
------------------------

It is a recipe kept in the upstream tree, under `contrib/`, so that it is
maintained beside the install set it claims to install. Rationale:
`docs/design/packaging.md` §6.3 — a recipe that lives only in a distribution's
own tree drifts from the install set with nothing reporting it.

It is not an official Debian package. No ITP has been filed, nobody upstream
maintains one, and `debian/control`'s `Maintainer:` is a deliberately
undeliverable placeholder. Replace it before uploading anywhere.

Rolling the orig tarball
------------------------

Build from `v0.4.1` or later. The two earlier tags are unusable for unrelated
reasons and neither announces it; `contrib/README.md` explains both.

    git archive --prefix=xtgeoip-0.4.1/ -o ../xtgeoip_0.4.1.orig.tar.gz v0.4.1

Use `git archive`, not `tar czf`. `export-ignore` in `.gitattributes` is an
attribute `git archive` consults and nothing else does; a tarball rolled by
copying a working tree silently reincludes `extra/`, which carries third-party
GPL-2 xtables-addons source that `debian/copyright` does not account for.

Then:

    tar xf ../xtgeoip_0.4.1.orig.tar.gz
    cd xtgeoip-0.4.1
    cp -r contrib/debian debian
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

`debian/source/format` is `3.0 (quilt)` rather than `3.0 (native)`, and the
distinction is not cosmetic here. `dpkg-source` does not read `.gitignore`: a
native build over a git working tree would tar `private/` and `scripts/`,
neither of which is tracked and neither of which may be published. Building
from the orig tarball above removes the question entirely — those paths are not
in git, so `git archive` cannot emit them.

Dependencies, and the buildd problem
------------------------------------

`Cargo.lock` has 307 crates and none of them come from Debian. Two ways to feed
the build:

  * **Network available** (a local build, or a build machine you control).
    Nothing more to do. `debian/rules` resolves over the network.

  * **No network** (a real buildd, and `sbuild`/`pbuilder` by default). Run
    `cargo vendor` in the unpacked source before `dpkg-buildpackage`.
    `debian/rules` detects `vendor/` and adds `--offline` to both the build and
    the test step, and writes the source-replacement config under
    `debian/cargo_home/` so that nothing outside `debian/` changes.

    `cargo vendor` expands to roughly 185 MB. If you put it in the orig tarball
    rather than generating it after unpacking, every vendored crate becomes part
    of the source package and `debian/copyright` has to account for all 307 —
    see the `Comment:` field there.

The build is `cargo build --release --locked`. The `--locked` is load-bearing
and not a style preference: six crates on the credential path (`argon2`,
`chacha20poly1305`, `secrecy`, `zeroize`, `toml_edit`, `serde-saphyr`) are
exact-pinned because an encrypted blob sits in `/etc/xtgeoip.conf`
indefinitely, in whatever format those crates produced on the day it was
written. A version bump there breaks no build and fails no test; it makes every
existing blob undecryptable. That is also why this recipe does not use
`dh --buildsystem=cargo`, which re-resolves by design.

`rust-toolchain.toml` pins 1.98.0. Debian's `cargo` is not rustup and ignores
that file, which is what you want on a build machine. If you test-build with a
rustup toolchain in the source directory it will honour the pin and fetch
1.98.0 — expected, not a fault of this recipe. `Build-Depends` asks for
`rustc (>= 1.85)` because the crate is edition 2024; that is the edition's
floor, not a tested MSRV, and upstream declares none.

Tests
-----

`debian/rules` runs `cargo test`, the hermetic suite: no root and no network.
`DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck` skips it.

It also writes nothing into the source tree, which matters more here than it
looks: under `3.0 (quilt)` anything a test left behind would surface on the
second `dpkg-source` pass as an unexplained change to upstream source, and fail
the build in a way that reads like a packaging bug. Checked rather than assumed
— `cargo test` followed by `git status --porcelain` leaves the tree clean.
`debian/rules clean` removes the only two things a build does create,
`debian/cargo_home/` and `target/`.

It does *not* run `xtgeoip-tests`, the integration suite, and should not: that
one needs root, a release build, `openssl(1)`, and it prompts for the
credentials passphrase on a terminal once per remote case.

Known deviations from what lintian expects
------------------------------------------

`/usr/share/doc/xtgeoip/LICENSE` duplicates `debian/copyright`, and lintian
flags a shipped licence file as redundant. It is here because the install set
is declared once, in `docs/spec/install.yaml`, and this recipe consumes that
declaration rather than curating it — dropping the entry here is exactly the
per-format divergence §6.3 exists to prevent. If you upload, add a
`debian/xtgeoip.lintian-overrides` carrying the tag your lintian actually
emits. Upstream does not ship one: asserting a tag name that nobody here can
run lintian to confirm is how such a file goes stale.

No `xtgeoip-dbgsym` is produced. `debian/rules` explains why in the comment
above `override_dh_strip`, and how to get one.

Cross-building is not handled. `debian/rules` calls `cargo build` without
`--target`, so `dpkg-buildpackage -a<arch>` will produce a host-architecture
binary. Native builds on each architecture work.

Keeping this in step with upstream
----------------------------------

`debian/changelog`'s version has to be bumped alongside `Cargo.toml`'s. That is
a restatement, and it is the only one in this recipe; it is pinned by
`tests::debian_changelog_version_matches_crate` in `src/bin/xtgeoip-docgen.rs`,
which fails the build if the two drift.

Everything else about what gets installed comes from
`docs/generated/install-manifest.tsv` and needs no edit here when the install
set changes.
